[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-8-1-dyn-5",
    "slot_key": "Initialization and status visibility requirements",
    "slot_value": "Reliable initialization and clear status reporting are important for getting the system into operation successfully; before go-live, the temperature sensor, equipment interface, configured thresholds, and alert contacts must all be in place and verified. The system should initialize successfully and show that it can read conditions and recognize equipment availability. On startup, it should confirm that temperature sensors are connected and returning believable readings, verify that the heating and cooling equipment it depends on is known and reachable, and verify its own configuration, especially any temperature limits or control rules, before making decisions. If anything is missing or out of range, it should report that clearly and stay in a safe not-ready state instead of pretending it’s operating normally. It should also confirm that sensors are present, responding, and giving values inside a believable range, that equipment or external command signals are reachable and reporting a valid status, and that missing, stale, or contradictory inputs cause normal control to be held off with an explicit report. When a device or input becomes unavailable or inconsistent during normal operation, it should clearly flag the problem right away and show which device or input has gone bad, while continuing only if it can do so safely; if the missing or inconsistent input affects control confidence, it should fall back to a safe degraded mode and tell the operator that manual attention is needed, without quietly guessing or acting as if the data were still good.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-4-3-3",
    "slot_key": "Fallback action when control cannot be executed",
    "slot_value": "The system should clearly flag the problem right away, show which device or input has gone bad, continue only if it can do so safely, and if the missing or inconsistent input affects control confidence it should fall back to a safe degraded mode and tell the operator that manual attention is needed. It should not quietly guess or keep acting as if the data were still good.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-3-2-1",
    "slot_key": "Status information displayed to users",
    "slot_value": "Review its status; see whether the system is running normally, whether something is out of range, whether equipment is unavailable, and review status and events for heating and cooling handling. Users also expect plain-language terms such as heating, cooling, temperature, equipment available/unavailable, limit reached, equipment failure, and mode change, used consistently so the same status/message means the same thing every time. The system also needs clear status reporting and must still tell users when heating or cooling equipment isn’t responding. From an operator’s point of view, it should clearly flag the problem right away and show which device or input has gone bad.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-3-2-dyn-5",
    "slot_key": "Inactive or reset safe state behavior",
    "slot_value": "When inactive or reset, the system should enter a safe, neutral state, make no active heating or cooling requests, keep monitoring only as needed to confirm safe resumption, report that it is not controlling the equipment, and remain inactive if sensor or equipment status is uncertain until the uncertainty clears. It should wait for valid temperature readings, confirmed equipment availability, and the normal startup check before becoming active again. If the reset happened because of a fault, it should stay inactive until the fault is cleared and acknowledged, not just until the temperature looks okay again. It should also remain inactive if the equipment keeps dropping in and out or if the readings are still unstable. If the missing or inconsistent input affects control confidence, it should fall back to a safe degraded mode and tell the operator that manual attention is needed. It should continue only if it can do so safely and should not quietly guess or keep acting as if the data were still good.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-3-3-3",
    "slot_key": "Event notification and acknowledgement behavior",
    "slot_value": "Alert operations staff right away when a limit is reached or equipment is unavailable; repeat the alert based on severity, with urgent temperature or equipment faults repeating sooner than minor warnings; escalate to a supervisor or maintenance contact if it remains unacknowledged past a defined timeout or if the condition gets worse; exact repeat intervals and contacts are set by operations and may vary by site; keep reporting the equipment as unavailable or not responding, and make it clear that temperature may be outside the desired range but the system is limited because the equipment can't respond; clearly flag the problem right away and show which device or input has gone bad; if missing or inconsistent input affects control confidence, fall back to a safe degraded mode and tell the operator that manual attention is needed; do not quietly guess or keep acting as if the data were still good",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-5-2-3",
    "slot_key": "External dependency failure signaling",
    "slot_value": "During normal operation and when supervised equipment is unavailable, disconnected, or not responding, the system should make clear that the equipment cannot be controlled or confirmed. When inactive or reset, it should also report that it is not controlling the equipment at the moment, and if there is any uncertainty about sensor readings or equipment status, it should stay inactive until that clears. It should keep evaluating temperature itself and keep reporting the equipment as unavailable or not responding, so people know the control action can’t be carried out. It should stop trying to command that equipment, or only retry at a reasonable interval, rather than constantly hammering it. It should also make it clear that temperature may be outside the desired range but the system is limited because the equipment can’t respond. If anything is missing or out of range at startup, it should report that clearly and stay in a safe not-ready state instead of pretending it’s operating normally. From the operator’s point of view, it should clearly flag the problem right away and show which device or input has gone bad, while continuing only if it can do so safely. If the missing or inconsistent input affects control confidence, it should fall back to a safe degraded mode and tell the operator that manual attention is needed. It should not quietly guess or keep acting as if the data were still good.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-6-2-2",
    "slot_key": "Hardware or platform constraints",
    "slot_value": "Must support reliable initialization, clear status reporting, and detection of non-responding heating or cooling equipment; should be straightforward for staff to use without much technical setup or constant attention. Operational priority for changes is safety and correct operation first, then availability, then delivery speed; low-risk configuration changes should keep the system available and be completed as quickly as possible, while changes that might cause false heating or cooling decisions should be paused or slowed down. When a device or input becomes unavailable or inconsistent during normal operation, the system should clearly flag the problem right away, identify which device or input has failed, continue only if it can do so safely, and fall back to a safe degraded mode with manual attention required if control confidence is affected; it should not quietly guess or keep acting as if the data were still good.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-6-2-dyn-4",
