    "slot_number": "slot-5-1-dyn-5",
    "slot_key": "Maintenance update and change management without operational disruption",
    "slot_value": "Routine updates may include threshold adjustments, contact changes, sensor or equipment replacements, software updates, and calibration changes. These should happen without interrupting normal monitoring if possible, or with only a short, planned maintenance window. The system should keep current operating settings stable until the change is confirmed so day-to-day operations are not disrupted unexpectedly. Responsibility should usually sit with facilities or operations, since they watch building conditions day to day, while maintenance or an engineer should approve changes that affect equipment behavior or safety thresholds. The responsibility should be clear so changes are not made casually. The maintenance window should be kept as short as possible based on the risk of leaving the system partly unavailable, and simple configuration edits should keep monitoring fully live; changes touching the sensor, control interface, or core software may require a brief pause to avoid bad readings or false alerts. Changes to minimum on/off timing settings should use a controlled maintenance path rather than everyday operators, with every change recorded by who made it and when. Operators should be able to see the currently active values in the status or settings view, along with enough history to tell when a timing rule was last changed. The system should clearly indicate whether a change takes effect immediately or only after restart or reinitialization.",
